re referring to mathematical analysis and the image will change: former students will then recall memories of derivatives and integrals, and no doubt remind you that it was much easier to calculate the former than the latter… But perhaps one might ask you: Mathematical analysis, no doubt it's all very nice, but whatapos;s its point? In fact, what are you analysing?\n\nThe book of Denis Choimet and Hervé Queffélec provides brilliant and profound answers to these questions in a most agreeable manner.We follow the evolution of analysis throughout the twentieth century, from the founding fathers Hardy and Littlewood, to the creators of spaces Wiener and Banach and up through contemporaries such as Lennart Carleson. The historical perspective helps us understand the motivation behind the problems, and the naturalness of their solutions. Moreover, analysis is shown clearly for what it is: a discipline situated in the heart of mathematics, indissolubly linked to arithmetic and number theory, to combinatorics, to probability theory, to logic, to geometry… Its objective is hence to serve mathematics and consequently all of the sciences, and thereby each and every one of us.\n\nI have the pleasure of knowing Denis and Hervé. Hence I can assert that their knowledge of analysis can be qualified as encyclopaedic. However, they were not attempting to write an encyclopaedia, and the roots of their work can be found more in Cambridge than in Paris, Warsaw or Moscow. This wise approach allowed them to explore multiple directions right up to the most recent results, while maintaining the profound unity of a very reasonably formatted book, providing constant encouragement to the reader.\n\n* * *\n\n## Information\n\nType\n    Chapter\n\nInformation\n    \n\nTwelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis , pp. xi - xii\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107445017.001 [Opens in a new window]\n\nPublisher: Cambridge University Press\n\nPrint publication year: 2015\n\n* * *\n\n  * Foreword\n  *     * By Gilles Godefroy\n  * D. Choimet, H. Queffélec, Université de Lille\n  * Book: Twelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis\n  * Online publication: 05 August 2015\n  * Chapter DOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107445017.001\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCambridge University Press & Assessment (https://assets.cambridge.org/97811070/59450/frontmatter/9781107059450_frontmatter.pdf)\nciteturn635search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; 978-1-107-05945-0 — Twelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis ... Choimet , H. ... Twelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis ... © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment\nCambridge University Press & Assessment\n978-1-107-05945-0 — Twelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis\nD. Choimet , H. Queffélec\nFrontmatter\nMore Information\nTwelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis\nThe striking theorems showcased in this book are among the most profound results\nof twentieth-century analysis. The authors’ original approach combines rigorous\nmathematical proofs with commentary on the underlying ideas to provide a rich insight\ninto these landmarks in mathematics. Results ranging from the proof of Littlewood’s\nconjecture to the Banach–Tarski paradox have been selected for their mathematical\nbeauty as well as their educative value and historical role. Placing each theorem in his-\ntorical perspective, the authors paint a coherent picture of modern analysis and its devel-\nopment, whilst maintaining mathematical rigour with the provision of complete proofs,\nalternative proofs, worked examples, and more than 150 exercises and solution hints.\nThis edition extends the original French edition of 2009 with a new chapter on\npartitions, including the Hardy–Ramanujan theorem, and a signiﬁcant expansion of the\nexisting chapter on the corona problem.\nwww.cambridge.org\n© in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis - D. Choimet, H. Queffélec - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books?id=KUBYCgAAQBAJ)\nciteturn635search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Twelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis ... Queffélec Cambridge University Press, Jul 15, 2015 - Mathematics The striking theorems showcased in this book are among the most profound results of twentieth-century analysis. ... Choimet has spent all of his academic career in the French lasses Prparatoires', an intensive two-year undergraduate programme leading to a nation-wide competitive examination for enrolment in one of the randes coles'. ... Quefflec has served on the committees for selecting secondary school Professors (Agrgation), and for hiring University researchers.\n\n# Twelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis\n\nD. Choimet, H. QueffélecCambridge University Press, Jul 15, 2015 - MathematicsThe striking theorems showcased in this book are among the most profound results of twentieth-century analysis. The authors' original approach combines rigorous mathematical proofs with commentary on the underlying ideas to provide a rich insight into these landmarks in mathematics. Results ranging from the proof of Littlewood's conjecture to the Banach–Tarski paradox have been selected for their mathematical beauty as well as educative value and historical role. Placing each theorem in historical perspective, the authors paint a coherent picture of modern analysis and its development, whilst maintaining mathematical rigour with the provision of complete proofs, alternative proofs, worked examples, and more than 150 exercises and solution hints. This edition extends the original French edition of 2009 with a new chapter on partitions, including the Hardy–Ramanujan theorem, and a significant expansion of the existing chapter on the Corona problem. \n### About the author (2015)\n\nD. Choimet has spent all of his academic career in the French lasses Prparatoires', an intensive two-year undergraduate programme leading to a nation-wide competitive examination for enrolment in one of the randes coles'. He currently teaches at the Lyce du Parc in Lyon, preparing students for the coles Normales Suprieures, the cole Polytechnique and many graduate engineering schools. Choimet is also a member of the jury of the grgation', a competitive examination leading to professorship positions.\n\nH. Quefflec shared his academic career between the universities of Paris-Sud and later Lille, where he is now an Emeritus Professor. He has written around fourty research papers in harmonic analysis and related probabilistic or topological methods, as well as in number theory (Dirichlet series) and operator theory, more specifically, composition operators and their approximation numbers. He has also written five textbooks and a research book on Banach spaces and Probabilistic methods (in collaboration with D. Li). Quefflec has served on the committees for selecting secondary school Professors (Agrgation), and for hiring University researchers. He was also a member of the CNU (National Council of Universities in France) which deals with the promotion of University members.\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | Twelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis\nAuthors  | D. Choimet, H. Queffélec\nPublisher  | Cambridge University Press, 2015\nISBN  | 1316352137, 9781316352137\nSubjects  | Mathematics › Mathematical Analysis\nMathematics / Mathematical Analysis\n   |  \nExport Citation  | BiBTeX EndNote RefMan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContents - Twelve Landmarks of Twentieth-Century Analysis (https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/twelve-landmarks-of-twentiethcentury-analysis/contents/54BD3BAC2A20C6118EAA12D1A18B56D6)\nciteturn635search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.2 years ago; Cra